/T (13. Explain the lifecycle management of data in ADLS.)
/E (13. Explain the lifecycle management of data in ADLS.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Data lifecycle management in Azure Data Lake Storage \(ADLS\) helps automate the process of storing, archiving, and deleting data efficiently. When data is first ingested, it's placed in a storage tier like Hot for frequently accessed information. As the data becomes less used over time, lifecycle policies can automatically move it to Cool or Archive tiers, which are cheaper but slower to access. You can set rules to define when data should be moved between these tiers based on how old it is or how often it's accessed. Retention policies can also be applied to automatically delete data after a certain period, helping manage storage costs and ensure compliance with data retention regulations. ADLS supports encryption to keep data secure, and versioning allows you to keep track of changes to files. Finally, after its useful life, the data is permanently deleted, ensuring efficient use of storage resources.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (14. How would you optimize data partitioning in ADLS for performance?)
/E (14. How would you optimize data partitioning in ADLS for performance?)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Optimizing data partitioning in Azure Data Lake Storage \(ADLS\) for performance involves structuring your data in a way that improves query speed and reduces costs. The key strategies:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 571 0 R
/ActualText (Partition by Relevant Columns: Partition data based on frequently queried columns, like date, region, or category. This allows queries to target specific subsets of data, reducing the amount of data scanned.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 572 0 R
/ActualText (Use Hierarchical Folder Structures: Organize your data in a folder hierarchy that mirrors your partitioning strategy \(e.g., /year=2024/month=10/region=US\). This helps tools like Azure Data Factory \(ADF\) and Azure Databricks efficiently access relevant partitions.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 573 0 R
/ActualText (Optimize File Sizes: Aim for optimal file sizes \(usually between 100 MB and 1 GB\) to balance between the overhead of handling small files and the inefficiency of very large files. This ensures better parallelism during processing.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 574 0 R
/ActualText (Avoid Over-Partitioning: Avoid creating too many small partitions, as it can lead to excessive metadata management and longer query times. Strike a balance between granularity and performance.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 575 0 R
/ActualText (Leverage Partition Pruning: Use query tools that support partition pruning, like Spark or Synapse, which skip unnecessary partitions based on query filters, improving query speed.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 576 0 R
/ActualText (Compression: Apply columnar file formats like Parquet or ORC with compression \(e.g., Snappy\) to reduce storage costs and speed up data scans.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 577 0 R
/ActualText (Regular Data Reorganization: Periodically reorganize partitions to merge small files and optimize query performance as data grows over time.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (These strategies will help ensure that your data in ADLS is efficiently partitioned for both storage and performance optimization.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
